# The Security and Syntax Traps in Custom Table Queries

When extending a WordPress site with custom database tables, the temptation to reach for raw SQL is strong. It feels direct: you have an ID, you know the table name, and you want a specific column value. However, the code snippet provided in your source material contains two critical flaws that will either break your application or expose it to severe security risks.

The primary issue lies in how the `$industry` variable is handled before being injected into the query string. In WordPress, we rely on prepared statements to separate data from SQL commands. This mechanism tells the database engine exactly what part of the query is code and what part is user input. By concatenating `$_REQUEST['industry']` directly into the string `"SELECT authority FROM $wpdb->tbl_industries WHERE id=$industry"`, you are creating an open injection vector. If a user inputs `1 OR 1=1` or attempts to read system tables, your query logic will collapse, and potentially return unauthorized data.

Furthermore, there is a subtle but dangerous syntax error in the table name interpolation. The line `$wpdb->tbl_industries` assumes that `tbl_industries` is a property of the global `$wpdb` object. In standard WordPress installations, custom tables are not automatically registered as properties on the `$wpdb` instance. Unless you have explicitly created this property in your plugin or theme initialization, accessing it via `$wpdb->tbl_industries` will result in a fatal error stating that the property does not exist. You must define the table name as a string constant or a class property, rather than expecting WordPress to know about it automatically.

Here is how you should structure the query correctly using `get_var()` and prepared statements. This approach ensures that the ID is treated strictly as data, never as executable code.

```php
global $wpdb;

// Define your custom table name clearly, not as a dynamic property unless initialized.
$table_name = 'your_plugin_tbl_industries'; // Replace with your actual table prefix and name

// Sanitize and prepare the value, though get_var() handles escaping internally for prepared statements.
$industry_id = sanitize_text_field( $_REQUEST['industry'] );

// Use placeholder syntax to prevent SQL injection
$query = "SELECT authority FROM {$table_name} WHERE id = %d";

// Execute the query safely
$authority = $wpdb->get_var( $wpdb->prepare( $query, $industry_id ) );

if ( $authority ) {
    echo '<p>' . esc_html( $authority ) . '</p>';
} else {
    // Handle cases where no row is found
    echo '<p>No industry data found for the selected ID.</p>';
}
```

Using `%d` in the query string explicitly tells WordPress that the parameter should be treated as an integer. This forces a type conversion before execution, which adds another layer of defense against malicious input strings. If you attempt to fetch a string column instead, you would switch to `%s`, though for an ID, integers are standard.

There is also a consideration regarding data retrieval efficiency and error handling. Always check if `$authority` returns a value before echoing it. In production environments, failing silently can lead to confusing user experiences. By checking the result, you can provide fallback content or display a "Not Found" message, ensuring the UI remains robust even when the database query returns `NULL`.

If you are building a complex application with multiple custom tables and need to manage them more cleanly, consider leveraging an ORM like Eloquent (available in Laravel) or a dedicated WordPress plugin that abstracts these queries. While raw SQL offers granular control, it shifts the entire burden of security and schema management onto your code. For most cases, sticking to `WPDB` methods with prepared statements is the industry standard for maintaining security without sacrificing performance.

For developers looking to explore more advanced patterns on managing custom tables or integrating external data sources securely, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) offer insights into how modern frameworks handle database relationships and migrations, though in the WordPress ecosystem, adhering to `WPDB` best practices remains the priority.
